# session entry.
#############################################

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_26_WhyCommunityFails a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Why 'Community' Fails: Everyone Wants a Village, Nobody Wants to Be a Villager"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by Nora Bateson (creator of Warm Data Labs) and her International Bateson Institute colleagues Jonathan Goldsmith and Lucas Jackson for a conversation about why so many attempts at community fail. Drawing on Bateson's concept of 'communing' as the necessary precursor to community, the group argues genuine human connection is being undermined by algorithmic fragmentation, scripted discourse, and transactional/individualist culture — and that the way out isn't a formula but older practices: mutual learning, radical hospitality, and relating outside one's perceived social role."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-04-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NoraBateson, tgs:Persona.JonathanGoldsmith, tgs:Persona.LucasJackson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-26> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/why-community-fails-everyone-wants> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Communing, tgs:Concept.Symmathesy, tgs:Concept.Schismogenesis, tgs:SchoolOfThought.WarmData, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.RadicalHospitality, tgs:Concept.Amonging, tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Different in character from RR-1 and RR-24's convergence-through-complementary-expertise — here the three guests are colleagues at the same institute, with Nora as the clear intellectual center (founder, coiner of the terms being discussed) and Jonathan and Lucas mostly contributing illustrative, experiential texture (a son's skating community, tea-making, a shared meal ritual, a teenage trip to see the Dalai Lama) rather than distinct or competing positions. What makes this episode distinct is that the conversation doesn't just describe the practice it's about — unscripted, non-competitive, deeply attentive listening — it visibly performs it; Nate explicitly notices this happening in real time more than once, remarking that none of the guests give him an immediate reflexive answer and that the best parts of the conversation were when he 'forgot I was a podcast host.' The closest thing to genuine friction is Nate's own pointed challenge — if a community selects for openness, curiosity, integrity, and generosity, wouldn't it simply get exploited or outcompeted by people with Dark Triad traits? — which the three guests work through collaboratively rather than by disagreeing with each other, building out Jonathan's 'lattice work' image together (bad-faith actors can't get purchase because the group's structure keeps moving). Nate also brings real personal vulnerability into the conversation himself, using his own unresolved dishes-with-a-partner example, which Nora then uses live to illustrate 'systems holdback' (a form of schismogenesis) rather than treating it as a hypothetical. The episode closes with two real, direct corroborations of facts already in this graph: Nora naming her new book, Belly, and confirming that she's appeared on the show more than anyone except Daniel Schmachtenberger."@en ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_10_TheEcologyOfCommunication, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.Belly, tgs:Work.Combining ;
    skos:scopeNote "RESOLVED 2026-08-13, via MJSullivan's own real extracted link list from this episode's real show notes: RadicalHospitality and Amonging both now built as real Concepts (were previously judged borderline in this same scopeNote), Dark Triad (RR-19) and the schismogenesis Reality Roundtable (RR-10) both now built as real Episode stubs (were previously flagged deferred). Daniel Schmachtenberger's own further TGS appearances and Nora Bateson's own extensive further TGS history remain real but still deferred beyond what's captured here."@en ;
    .

