tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_27_AnEcologicalPsychology a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "An Ecological Psychology: Internal Family Systems for a Turbulent World"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by Richard Schwartz, creator of Internal Family Systems (IFS) — the therapeutic model describing the mind as naturally composed of 'parts' (exiles, managers, firefighters) organized around an undamageable core 'Self' — and climate psychologist Steffi Bednarek, who extends the same model outward to organizations, governments, and whole cultures. The conversation moves from IFS fundamentals through Nate's own explicit application of the model to his 'economic Superorganism' framing, to Bednarek's real work running Systems Wayfinding sessions with governments and wealth holders, and closes on the spiritual/non-dual dimension of the 'Self' both guests have independently arrived at."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-08-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.RichardSchwartz, tgs:Persona.SteffiBednarek ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-27> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/RR-27-Transcript-An-Ecological-Psychology.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti ;
    skos:scopeNote "Unusually rich in real, direct cross-references to already-built content in this graph: the episode's own real show notes cite Frankly_97 ('The Superorganism in 7 Minutes') by name for the Economic Superorganism concept, RR-19 by name for the Dark Triad discussion, and Vanessa Andreotti's own real prior TGS episode (#125, via the term 'Hospicing Modernity') — all three already real, built individuals in this graph before this episode was processed. Also real and directly confirmed: Francis Weller's own prior TGS episode (#167) and Xiye Bastida's (#208), both cited directly by Nate from personal experience but not built as Episode individuals in this pass. Frankly #123 ('The Consumption Pyramid') is directly confirmed as the completed version of the 'Frankly on public choice' Nate says he is 'working on' mid-conversation — real evidence this episode predates that Frankly's publication."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The longest and most extended conversation among all built RRs — Nate's own words near the close: 'coming up on two hours, this is one of the longer conversations I've had this year.' Schwartz and Bednarek are visibly not just co-guests but active collaborators already deep in an ongoing dialogue of their own ('this is a conversation that I am currently having with Dick and I really love it,' Bednarek says at one point) — the complementary split is genuinely structural rather than just topical: Schwartz supplies the clinical, individual-level IFS framework directly, while Bednarek supplies its real extension into organizations, governments, and whole cultures via her own Systems Wayfinding work. The one moment of real, pointed challenge in the episode comes from Nate himself, not between the guests — he directly names the skepticism his own audience is likely to feel toward psychological frameworks given the podcast's usual material/energy/systems focus, asking bluntly how IFS could actually affect a trajectory driven by biophysical limits rather than individual psychology. Both guests take the challenge seriously rather than deflecting: Bednarek is explicit that 'no psychological approach can stop the unfolding of biodiversity loss that is already underway,' reframing the value as being about how humans meet what's coming rather than preventing it; Schwartz answers instead with his own long-term, admittedly 'grandiose' goal of IFS replacing the dominant paradigm for relating to the mind entirely. The episode's most candid moment is Schwartz's own disclosed intellectual and spiritual evolution, told live rather than summarized: he began the work as a self-described 'scientific atheist' and has only this year become openly willing to describe the IFS 'Self' as a 'particle state' of something larger that different spiritual traditions have independently pointed to under different names. Nate's own participation is unusually reflexive throughout — he explicitly narrates applying the framework to himself in real time ('I feel like I've been in self-energy since this conversation started'), and Schwartz risks a genuinely vulnerable, self-deprecating aside about being triggered by his own wife, fully aware she would later watch the recording."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — RR-6 "Fossil Free Food Systems," sourced from a
# complete real transcript + real linked show notes (MJSullivan's own
# upload, plus a direct fetch of the episode's own real page for the
# table).
#############################################

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_6_FossilFreeFoodSystems a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fossil Free Food Systems: Jason Bradford, Andrew Millison, Vandana Shiva, Daniel Zetah"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate reunites four returning guests — farmer/biologist Jason Bradford (Post Carbon Institute), permaculture educator Andrew Millison (Oregon State University), physicist-turned-agroecology-activist Vandana Shiva (Navdanya), and Minnesota regenerative farmer Daniel Zetah — around one central question: could 8+ billion people be fed with minimal fossil fuel inputs, and what would that actually require in labor, land, culture, and policy? The conversation moves from de-urbanization and village-scale redesign through Shiva's real longitudinal soil-nutrition data to closing visions of each guest's own bioregion in 2050."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-10-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-11-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JasonBradford, tgs:Persona.AndrewMillison, tgs:Persona.VandanaShiva, tgs:Persona.DanielZetah ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr06-bradford-millison-shiva-zetah> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/RR06BradfordMillisonShivaZetahTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    skos:scopeNote "William Rees is directly cross-referenced by Bradford ('a friend of yours, Nate, and mine') via the exact same real prior-episode YouTube URL already used for tgs:Persona.WilliamRees during this session's RR-2 build (https://youtu.be/LQTuDttP2Yg) — independent confirmation, not a new fact. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: real later solo TGS appearances for both Bradford (2026, Farming Clubs) and Millison (TGS #178, 2025); Bradford's real prior episode #76 is shared with Zetah's own prior appearance (a joint episode, not two separate ones — worth flagging as a real, unusual case of two guests sharing one numbered prior episode, though not verified further in this pass)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Highly convergent — Jason Bradford's own mid-episode summary names it directly: 'I think we're all kind of singing the same song here.' The one moment of genuine, explicit reframing rather than agreement comes from Daniel Zetah, who opens his turn by directly challenging Nate's own question: 'I would actually like to just challenge the question itself' — arguing the premise that the current fossil-fueled system is even 'feeding the world' well is itself false, given documented malnutrition and obesity coexisting under it. A second, milder real disagreement is one of degree rather than direction: Bradford's opening statement calls for depopulating cities outright, while Millison pushes back gently that 'not all cities are created equal,' pointing to cities historically sited on rivers and trade routes as potentially viable in a lower-energy future rather than uniformly abandoned. Shiva grounds the whole conversation in real, specific longitudinal data from her own farm (20 years of soil nitrogen, zinc, and manganese measurements comparing organic and chemical-input plots), giving the discussion an empirical anchor the other three build on rather than dispute. The episode's most self-aware moment is Bradford's own 'Shire' analogy via Lord of the Rings, which he flags himself as 'corny' before continuing anyway — Nate affectionately teases him for it at the close ('I knew you were corny') while praising his 'agro-ecological wisdom.' All four converge fully, without qualification, on ending agricultural subsidies and patents on seed/life as the necessary first policy steps — the clearest full consensus point in the episode."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — RR-25 "Why Standardized Testing Contributed to
# the Metacrisis," sourced from a complete real transcript + real
# linked show notes (MJSullivan's own upload, plus a direct fetch of
# the episode's own real page for the table). Zak Stein returns here
# for a second real built appearance in this graph (see RR-20).
#############################################

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_25_WhyStandardizedTestingContributedToTheMetacrisis a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Why Standardized Testing Contributed to the Metacrisis – and How to Fix It"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by developmental psychologist Theo Dawson (founder, Lectica) and returning guest Zak Stein — established real academic collaborators and co-founders who published together as far back as 2004 — to trace how 20th-century standardized testing (Army Alpha/Beta, the SAT, No Child Left Behind) narrowed education toward memorization and measurable-for-employers outcomes at the expense of what Dawson argues is real 'measurement' of mental development. The conversation moves from that historical account through Lectica's own real MindLog tool and closes on Zak Stein's argument, from his own paper 'Education is the Metacrisis,' that civilizational dysfunction is downstream of how a society raises its next generation."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TheoDawson, tgs:Persona.ZakStein ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-25> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/RR-25-Transcript-Learning-in-a-Way-that-Actually-Matters.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_20_HackingHumanAttachment, tgs:Persona.NoraBateson, tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Recorded the same day as the real 2026 Iran war/Strait of Hormuz crisis (March 25, 2026), directly acknowledged by Nate at the top of the conversation. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jonathan Haidt's own real prior TGS episode (#59) on youth mental health; Iain McGilchrist, already a real, built Persona in this graph, is directly cross-referenced again via 3 of his own real prior TGS episodes (#217, #165, #85) for his point about measurement destroying original beauty — the same point Nate raises early in this transcript, itself referencing a real conversation he'd had with McGilchrist 'yesterday' relative to this recording. Both Nora Bateson and Daniel Schmachtenberger, already real and built in this graph, are directly cross-referenced again by name (Bateson via 'Warm Data,' Schmachtenberger via the term 'generative dynamic')."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Dawson and Stein are not merely co-guests but established real academic collaborators — they co-published together as early as 2004, and Stein is himself a co-founder of Dawson's organization, Lectica. That history shows throughout: Stein consistently defers to Dawson on technical measurement detail while supplying the broader philosophical and civilizational framing, and at one point directly credits her as the origin of his own thinking ('the first part of my career was working with Theo'). The episode's central intellectual move is Dawson's own sharp distinction between 'testing' (what nearly all of 20th-century education actually did) and 'measurement' (a rarer, content-independent standard she argues her own Lectical Assessment System achieves) — a distinction she returns to repeatedly as the conversation's organizing frame. Nate draws an explicit bridge between the guests' education focus and his own usual energy/systems framing, asking Stein directly to connect Lectica's work to 'the broader meta-crisis' — Stein answers by invoking his own paper, 'Education is the Metacrisis,' and reframing civilizational purpose itself as a choice between accumulating abstract profit and regenerating the next generation's capacity. The one small, genuinely human moment is Stein's own self-deprecating aside about his dyslexia while trying to recall a historical name mid-sentence. Dawson's own most personal disclosure — living off-grid with her children for a significant stretch, and the jolt of re-encountering advertising and its effect on her self-image afterward — grounds the episode's abstract argument about embodied learning in a real, lived account rather than only theory. Nate closes by revealing the episode's own origin: a private, 1.5-hour conversation between him and Stein about AI and education that led directly to inviting Dawson onto the show."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — RR-7 "Poverty Blind," sourced from a complete
# real transcript + real linked show notes (MJSullivan's own upload,
# plus a direct fetch of the episode's own real page for the table).
#############################################

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_7_PovertyBlind a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Stephanie Hoopes, Peter Kilde, Marc Perry, Dalitso Sulamoyo: \"Poverty Blind\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate convenes four anti-poverty practitioners — Stephanie Hoopes (creator of the ALICE financial-hardship measure), Peter Kilde (West CAP, Wisconsin, and Nate's real-life neighbor with a working relationship dating to 2010), Marc Perry (Community Action Inc. of Rock and Walworth Counties), and Dalitso Sulamoyo (Champaign County Regional Planning Commission, Illinois) — to examine US poverty beyond the federal poverty line, using Hoopes's ALICE metric to show 41% of US households cannot afford a bare-minimum survival budget despite being employed, often in multiple jobs. The conversation moves through disaster-response inequality (Katrina, Maria), racial disparity, and closes on what community-level resilience can do as resource constraints from the Great Simplification deepen."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-11-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-12-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.StephanieHoopes, tgs:Persona.PeterKilde, tgs:Persona.MarcPerry, tgs:Persona.DalitsoSulamoyo ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr07-hoopes-kilde-perry-sulamoyo> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/RR07HoopesKildePerryandSulamoyoTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Paul Ehrlich (real TGS #9, not yet built as a Persona) and Dennis Meadows (real TGS #12 — Meadows already exists as a real, built Persona in this graph, cited here only in passing, not as a guest, so his own record was not touched in this pass)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Highly collaborative among four practitioners who mostly extend rather than contest each other's points, anchored throughout by Hoopes's own ALICE data (41% of US households below a survival budget despite employment). The clearest moment of real, pointed directness rather than diplomatic framing comes from Marc Perry, describing the frustration in Black communities at having raised the same structural concerns for decades to what he calls 'deaf ears' — 'it took somebody white to say it in order for you to actually pay attention to it.' A second genuinely candid moment, flagged by the speaker himself as sensitive, is Dalitso Sulamoyo raising real community tension over resource allocation to recent asylum seekers versus longtime residents — he calls it 'quite a touchy issue' and does not soften it further. Peter Kilde grounds the episode's abstract inequality argument in specific documented history — a detailed comparison of Hurricane Katrina and Hurricane Maria's radically unequal disaster responses, including real death tolls and recovery-fund disparities — building on his own extensive, real personal connection to New Orleans and a professional history with peak-oil and resource-depletion work (ASPO-USA board membership) that predates and directly parallels Nate's own subject matter. Marc Perry's own personal disclosure — growing up materially poor but 'rich' in family and community, not realizing he was poor until told — offers a lived counterpoint to the episode's data-heavy first half. The two guests' closing interpretive frames diverge memorably without conflicting: Kilde insists poverty is fundamentally 'a math problem,' not a behavioral one, while Perry frames the culture's complacency through Huxley's Brave New World rather than Orwell's 1984 — people appeased into indifference rather than terrorized into it."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — RR-8 "The Indic Mind," sourced from a complete
# real transcript + real linked show notes (MJSullivan's own upload,
# plus a direct fetch of the episode's own real page for the table).
#############################################

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_8_TheIndicMind a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Indic Mind: An Approach to the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by three Indian NGO leaders he met in person at a metacrisis symposium in Auroville less than 3 months before recording — Mohit Trivedi and Abhishek Thakore (co-founders, 2069 Ecosystem) and Kejal Savla (co-founder/CEO, Wisdom Tree) — to explore what they call the 'Indic mind': a distinctly non-Western, relationality-first, paradox-holding approach to the metacrisis, grounded in India's continuous spiritual and philosophical traditions rather than primarily technical or policy responses."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-04-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.MohitTrivedi, tgs:Persona.AbhishekThakore, tgs:Persona.KejalSavla ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-8> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/RR08TrivediThakoreSavlaTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    skos:scopeNote "The Superorganism/Moloch concept is directly translated cross-culturally in this transcript — Mohit Trivedi renders it in his own language as 'Shaitan,' a real, notable moment of conceptual bridge-building this graph's own English-language Concept definition doesn't otherwise capture. Real, direct prior professional connection: Kejal Savla served as Chief Functionary of Abhishek Thakore's Blue Ribbon Movement for years before co-founding Wisdom Tree — confirmed via independent search, not asserted in the transcript itself."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Distinctly different register from most other built RRs — philosophical and spiritual rather than technical, with real harmony among the three guests, who have spent years deliberately building what they call a 'relational field' with each other before ever discussing the metacrisis directly. The central original contribution is the 'Indic mind' itself, developed across the episode as a paradox-holding, relationality-first orientation distinct from Western crisis-response framing — Abhishek's own account of first meeting Nate is telling: they spent four hours talking about relationships and process without once mentioning climate or the metacrisis. The clearest moment of Nate's own assumption being corrected live comes when he directly states his expectation that Indians would feel resentment toward the Global North for disproportionate carbon emissions and climate impact — Abhishek and Mohit both describe something more layered: real awareness of the injustice without it curdling into blame, attributed to a cultural orientation toward self-directed agency rather than externalized grievance. Mohit's own account of living without air conditioning through 37-40°C heat by personal choice — while explicitly declining to 'glorify' the choice or claim it as a virtue — grounds the episode's more abstract philosophical claims in a concrete, lived practice. The one real point of divergence, held without resolution rather than smoothed into agreement, is each guest's relationship to hope: Kejal insists hope 'is not a choice... a stand that you take,' while Mohit says plainly 'hope is [an] illusioned word' and offers no answer for it, preferring confrontation with and embrace of the unknown instead. The episode's most emotionally exposed moment is Abhishek's own closing disclosure — 'it makes me very emotional to be honest' — describing his felt sense of a worldwide, unnamed network of people quietly doing this work, a claim he explicitly frames as testimony rather than argument."@en ;
    .

