# ADDED 2026-08-13 — RR-11 "Tales from the Carbon Pulse," sourced
# from a complete real transcript + real linked show notes
# (MJSullivan's own upload, plus a direct fetch of the episode's own
# real page for the table). Both guests are returning: Tom Murphy and
# DJ White previously appeared together in RR-24.
#############################################

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_11_TalesFromTheCarbonPulse a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tales from the Carbon Pulse: Tom Murphy, DJ White"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by two decades-long friends and intellectual collaborators — physicist Tom Murphy and Greenpeace/Earthtrust co-founder DJ White — for a wide-ranging examination of the ecological cost of the carbon pulse: the collapse of wild mammal biomass (from 50,000kg per person 10,000 years ago to 2.5kg today), human-built mass now exceeding all living biomass on Earth, the physical limits of 'rebuildable' energy, and two genuinely different theories — Nate's supply-side view versus Tom's newly-considered demographic-collapse view — of when and why global human power output will peak."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-08-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-10-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TomMurphy, tgs:Persona.DJWhite ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-11> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/RR11TranscriptTalesfromtheCarbonPulse.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_24_TheFantasyOfSpaceColonization, tgs:Persona.DennisMeadows ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode's own real show notes directly cite both tgs:Work.RealityBlind and tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury via the same reader-platform pattern, prompting this session's build of the latter as a real Work individual — resolving the second of DJ White's real 'three books' with Nate (a third remains unidentified; this episode's own transcript independently reconfirms the count of three without naming it, the same unresolved detail flagged during the RR-24 and RR-9 builds)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "An especially close, long-standing friendship — all three explicitly reference exchanging 'thousands of emails over the last 10 years' — but not one that suppresses real disagreement. The clearest substantive divergence: Tom Murphy proposes, newly and tentatively, that collapsing global fertility rates could drive 'peak civilizational power' as early as 2033 through demand-side contraction rather than supply-side resource limits — Nate directly pushes back ('I'm going to push back on that... I think that's going to be a much bigger constraint... than the demographics'), and DJ White, while finding Murphy's data 'a head spinner,' states plainly he still comes down on the side of supply constraints. None of the three resolve this into agreement; it's held open. A real, notable moment of live intellectual honesty: Murphy explicitly describes changing his own mind mid-conversation, calling the demographic argument something he'd 'never considered before' and being 'gobsmacked' by the fertility data. DJ White's own account of he and his wife's decision not to have more biological children — framed provocatively as 'DNA is essentially our enemy at this point' — is offered candidly as central to his own philosophy, not softened. White also introduces a real, distinctive naming device mid-conversation: naming the hypothetical trillionth human being 'Naya' as a rhetorical anchor for thinking in deep time about population ethics. Murphy's own disillusionment with academia is disclosed personally and specifically — recounting 'blank stares' from colleagues he considered geniuses when he tried to discuss civilizational limits, and citing this disappointment as a real factor in his decision to retire at 53. The recurring 'man rushing at you with a hammer' analogy, explicitly credited to Dennis Meadows, becomes the episode's central reframe of the renewable-energy debate — arguing the relevant question isn't which weapon (fossil vs. renewable) but why there's an attacker at all."@en ;
    .

