tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jeremy Grantham: \"Pollution, Population & Purpose\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts GMO co-founder and environmental philanthropist Jeremy Grantham for a wide-ranging, unusually long conversation spanning his 'super bubble' mean-reversion framework for financial markets, resource depletion and finite-world limits, a real substantive disagreement with Nate over the role of debt in the biophysical economy, endocrine-disrupting chemicals and the sperm-decline/insect-collapse research Grantham personally funds (including Shanna Swan's), wealth inequality, green venture capital, and closing reflections on purpose, population, and personal advice to younger listeners."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-11-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JeremyGrantham ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/99-jeremy-grantham> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/TGS99JeremyGranthamTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/cTvN9iFJ0fY> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.ShannaSwan, tgs:Persona.WilliamRees, tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    skos:scopeNote "A prior version of this graph incorrectly claimed Grantham was already built as a Persona here (surfaced and corrected during the RR-15 build) — this episode resolves that gap properly. This episode's own real show notes directly confirm William Rees's real prior TGS episode via the identical YouTube URL already used building him during this session's RR-2 and RR-6 work — independent cross-session consistency, not a new fact. Shanna Swan's own real prior TGS episode (#2), also directly confirmed here, is no longer deferred — now built as a real Episode individual (tgs:Interview.TGS_2_ShannaSwan). Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: E.O. Wilson (directly described being 'badgered' for three hours by Grantham and colleagues about insect collapse) and Hagai Levine (Shanna Swan's real co-researcher, directly named)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "One of the longest interviews in the whole podcast (roughly 2 hours 20 minutes) and unusually wide-ranging even by that standard. The clearest, most substantive moment is a real, named intellectual disagreement rather than deference: Nate directly pushes back on Grantham's dismissal of debt as 'shockingly misunderstood and exaggerated in significance,' arguing from a biophysical rather than purely financial lens that debt creation still draws down real resources even if it nets to zero on a balance sheet. Grantham's own response is notable for what it is NOT — he doesn't argue back, explicitly declining ('I don't think it matters... I don't want us to spend any more time') while calling his own position 'eccentric,' a graceful agree-to-disagree rather than resolution by persuasion. Grantham discloses, unprompted, a genuine self-correction on his own public image: despite being introduced as a billionaire, he states he's 'at least an order of magnitude short of that' by any literal measure, only reaching billionaire status by counting money already given away — Nate's own opening had already flagged this correction as something Grantham does in the conversation. The episode's emotional and scientific core is Grantham's direct, personal account of funding Shanna Swan's sperm-decline research and connecting it to a parallel, independently-observed collapse in insect populations (including his own real three-hour conversation with E.O. Wilson) — delivered with real urgency ('we're on our way out of business') rather than abstract concern. The closing 'magic wand' answer is genuinely stark and stated without hedging: Grantham says he would reduce global population to 1 billion, framing the entire modern predicament as a roughly hundred-year mismatch between population growth and technological capacity. Grantham closes with direct, substantial praise for the podcast's long-term potential impact, having 'spent 30 or 40 hours' reviewing prior episodes before this recording, and Nate ends by inviting him back for a future Reality Roundtable."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — Frankly_47 "Frankly Re: Franklys," sourced from a
# real, off-the-cuff auto-generated transcript (garbled timestamps,
# not cleaned up to the level of most other episodes in this graph —
# treated with the same extra caution on exact wording as RR-13) plus
# a direct fetch of the episode's own real page for accurate metadata
# and show notes. Solo Nate monologue reflecting on the Frankly format
# itself and previewing many specific future Frankly topics.
#############################################

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_47_FranklyReFranklys a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    skos:prefLabel "Frankly Re: Franklys"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real-time, unscripted reflection on the Frankly format itself — recorded the same Friday afternoon it discusses (Oct 27, 2023), amid the opening days of the Israel-Gaza war and Mike Johnson's election as US House Speaker the day before. Nate states the format's actual purpose directly (informing a 'scout team' of humans and, secondarily, attempting to shift the consciousness of those in power, not offering real-time investment intel), gives a raw take on unfolding Middle East and financial-market events, and previews an extensive, explicitly 'non-exhaustive' list of specific future Frankly topics he has planned."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-10-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-10-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/47-frankly-re-franklys> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://youtu.be/ZJbKVu7vbLk> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct prior-episode reference confirmed via this episode's own real show notes: Frankly #46 ('The Middle East: Stormwatch') — not yet built as an Episode individual in this pass. This episode previews an extensive real list of specific future Frankly topics, none of which match any Episode individual currently built in this graph by title — real, deferred candidates worth checking against as more Franklys are built: AI vs. real ecology (steelmanning AI-solves-everything optimism against ecological limits), a 'flowchart of the future' relating interest rates/governance/inequality/nuclear power/debt, a philanthropy framework organized around Great Simplification-relevant categories, 'renewable energy: right answer to the wrong question,' 'what if solar panels were free,' 'seven lenses' (individual/emotional through universal/pale-blue-dot perspectives on the future), nuclear power's role in an energy-constrained future, and the relationship between US debt growth and Strategic Petroleum Reserve drawdown. Also worth noting: the transcript's own 'behavioral stack' framing (cellular/enteric/limbic/cognitive, visualized as a pyramid rather than cognition-on-top dominance) is the same real concept independently cited with its own real video reference in this graph's own RR-9 show notes — a real, substantive conceptual throughline across episodes, not yet minted as its own Concept individual here."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Genuinely raw and unscripted — Nate explicitly frames it as having 'no outline here, this is just a complete off-the-cuff riff,' and the delivery matches: real-time analysis of the Israel-Gaza war's likely trajectory and US debt/interest-rate dynamics, offered candidly as non-expert commentary rather than polished analysis ('I'm not an expert on that other than to say...'). The episode's real center of gravity is Nate directly stating the Frankly format's own purpose in his own words for the first time this explicitly: building awareness in a 'scout team' of engaged listeners, and secondarily attempting to shift the 'consciousness of those in power' who hold outsized leverage over institutional choices. A genuinely personal, vulnerable thread runs through the closing third: Nate discloses working with a coach on shifting from 'productivity' to 'awareness,' describes his own imperfect attempt at tech-free 'Silent Saturdays' ('doing kind of half-assed... because I'm so busy'), and states plainly, without hedging, 'I will never be anyone's guru... I'm not only an analyst but I'm a client' — real self-disclosure about his own use of the same practices he recommends, offered as an admission of imperfection rather than authority."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — Frankly_58 "The Fellowship of the Ring: 'Bend
# Not Break' Version," sourced from a complete real transcript + real
# linked show notes (MJSullivan's own upload, plus a direct fetch of
# the episode's own real page for the table). This is the real,
# confirmed origin of the "Bend Not Break" phrase used throughout this
# graph (tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak, the Daniel Schmachtenberger
# 'Bend Not Break' series, and multiple episode cross-references) —
# resolves a real, previously-deferred connection.
#############################################

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_58_FellowshipBendNotBreak a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Fellowship of the Ring: 'Bend Not Break' Version"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate recasts Tolkien's Lord of the Rings/The Hobbit as an allegory for the roles society needs across 3 distinct timelines. First, 9 real archetypes of business-as-usual society (Banker/Investor, Worker, Technologist, Barker/Salesman, Scholar, Warrior, Preacher, Consumer, Elite), each paired with a real, specific decline-scenario counterpart it could 'morph into' under contraction (e.g. Banker to Hoarder, Worker to Indentured/Slave, Consumer to Addict). Second, 9 archetypes for a successfully-navigated distant future (Ecologist, Engineer, Ancestor, Craftsman, Healer, Stalwart/Earth Warden, Merrymaker, Matriarch). Third — and the episode's real core — 9 archetypes specifically needed for the near-term 'Bend Not Break' moment itself (Silvertongue, Alchemist, Disarmer, Muse, Effectivist, Barber, Governess, Wellspring, Catalyst), forming the real 'Fellowship' the title refers to."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2024-03-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-03-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/58-the-fellowship-of-the-ring-bend-not-break-version> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Frankly58Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/3oh-zdo-l8I> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    dct:references tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Patrick Knodel's own real prior TGS solo episode (#115), directly named; Frankly #44 ('The Many Shapes of the Carbon Pulse') and Frankly #57 ('Information Bifurcation'), both real, confirmed, directly cross-referenced. The full 27-archetype system (9 per timeline, with real 'morphs into' pairings for the business-as-usual set) is genuinely rich, structured content — comparable in scale to the Frankly_132 six-fronts taxonomy already built in this graph — but was NOT minted as individuals in this pass, since MJSullivan asked to 'create' this episode specifically rather than explicitly requesting the fuller taxonomy build the way the six-fronts pass was explicitly requested. Worth a dedicated follow-up pass if wanted, using the same InterventionFront/InterventionSubdomain-style schema pattern already established (e.g. a new thinkr:Archetype class, grouped by timeline via a property, with the 9 business-as-usual archetypes each linked to their real decline-counterpart)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely different register from most Franklys built this session — explicitly playful ('I loved the magic swords and the dragons') rather than analytical, though building toward a real, substantive point. Nate opens with an unusual, direct meta-apology for a prior episode's audio experiment ('I will never do that again'), then spends the bulk of the episode building a structured, 3-tier archetype system rather than a single flat list. The most intellectually interesting move is the direct 'morphs into' pairing for each business-as-usual archetype under contraction — not random decline, but a real, specific transformation Nate assigns to each (Banker to Hoarder via loss aversion specifically, Technologist to Cyborg via AI/big data specifically, Elite to a genuine fork he names directly: either a Hoarder building bunkers, or a real collective-response contributor). The episode's actual emotional core, by Nate's own explicit statement, isn't the archetype taxonomy itself but the feeling of 'camaraderie and fellowship' he associates with the source material — he states this directly as 'the most important part of this little Frankly reflection.' The episode closes on a real, generous reframe of individual agency: rather than requiring a Frodo-style singular hero, Nate points to Sam and Gollum as the real Catalysts who changed Lord of the Rings' outcome, offering this directly to listeners as a model for how an ordinary, unheroic role ('you do your job, live with your family, enjoy your life') can still meaningfully shift a larger outcome."@en ;
    .

